      </ol>
    </details>
  </nav>

  <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-h">
    <h2 id="what-h">What Is the Shoulder-to-Waist Ratio?</h2>
    <p>The shoulder-to-waist ratio compares the circumference of your shoulders with the circumference of your waist. A higher number means broader shoulders over a narrower waist: the V-shaped upper body that many men train for. It is often called the <strong>Adonis index</strong>, with the golden ratio of <strong>1.618</strong> as the ideal.</p>
    <figure class="tp-fig"><img src="/assets/images/shoulder-to-waist-ratio-chart-3d.webp" width="1600" height="980" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Shoulder-to-waist ratio chart: the same shoulders with ratios of 1.10, 1.26, 1.40 and 1.618"><figcaption>Same shoulders, four waists: ratios from 1.10 to the golden ratio of 1.618.</figcaption></figure>
    <p>The ratio is about appearance, not health. A good ratio and a healthy waist often go together, though: shrinking the waist improves both. For a health check, use the <a href="/waist-to-height-ratio-calculator/">waist-to-height ratio</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="chart" aria-labelledby="chart-h">
    <h2 id="chart-h">Shoulder-to-Waist Ratio Chart</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table"><caption>Shoulder-to-waist ratio bands for men</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Ratio</th><th scope="col">Rating</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Below 1.18</th><td>Below average</td><td>Waist close to shoulder size; little visible taper.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.18&ndash;1.34</th><td>Average</td><td>Middle half of U.S. Army men.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.34&ndash;1.46</th><td>Athletic</td><td>Clear taper; top quarter of men.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.46&ndash;1.6</th><td>Strong V-taper</td><td>Top 5% of men.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.618+</th><td>Golden ratio</td><td>Classic aesthetic ideal; rare without training and low body fat.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>The average comes from the U.S. Army ANSUR&nbsp;II survey (median 1.26, middle half 1.18&ndash;1.34). Soldiers are leaner than average, so civilian men usually score lower: the average U.S. man has a 40.6&nbsp;inch waist (see <a href="/average-waist-size-men/">average waist size for men</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="golden" aria-labelledby="golden-h">
    <h2 id="golden-h">The Golden Ratio Body: Targets by Waist Size</h2>
    <div class="tp-tablewrap"><table class="tp-table tp-num"><caption>Shoulders needed for a given ratio</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Ratio</th><th scope="col">Waist</th><th scope="col">Shoulders needed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1.618</th><td>32 in</td><td>51.8 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.618</th><td>34 in</td><td>55.0 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.618</th><td>36 in</td><td>58.2 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.45</th><td>34 in</td><td>49.3 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.35</th><td>34 in</td><td>45.9 in</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">1.26</th><td>36 in</td><td>45.4 in</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <p>Reaching 1.618 usually takes both broad shoulders and a lean waist. Because the waist is the smaller number, cutting it has a bigger effect: losing 2&nbsp;inches of waist raises the ratio about as much as adding 2.5&ndash;3&nbsp;inches of shoulder circumference.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="chest" aria-labelledby="chest-h">
    <h2 id="chest-h">Chest-to-Waist Ratio</h2>
    <p>The chest-to-waist ratio uses chest circumference instead of shoulders. It shows a similar V shape but reacts more to chest muscle and less to shoulder width. For men, about 1.1 shows a moderate taper, 1.25 a clear V shape and 1.4 a muscular build. Our <a href="/body-proportions-calculator/">body proportions calculator</a> works it out alongside waist-to-hip and leg-length ratios.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-to-measure" aria-labelledby="how-to-measure-h">
    <h2 id="how-to-measure-h">How to Measure Shoulders and Waist</h2>
    <ol class="tp-steps"><li><strong>Measure your shoulders.</strong> Stand relaxed with arms at your sides. Have someone wrap a tape around the widest part of your shoulders and upper arms, over the deltoids, keeping it level.</li><li><strong>Measure your waist.</strong> Wrap the tape around your waist at the navel, level with the floor, after a normal breath out. Do not suck in.</li><li><strong>Divide.</strong> Divide the shoulder measurement by the waist measurement, using the same unit for both.</li><li><strong>Add your chest (optional).</strong> Measure around the fullest part of the chest under the arms to get chest-to-waist ratio as well.</li></ol>
    <p>Note that shoulder <em>circumference</em> is different from shoulder <em>width</em> measured straight across the back. For width, see <a href="/average-shoulder-width/">average shoulder width</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="women" aria-labelledby="women-h">
    <h2 id="women-h">Shoulder-to-Waist Ratio for Women</h2>
    <p>Women&rsquo;s shoulders are narrower and their waists sit higher, so the same numbers mean different things. Large surveys do not publish this ratio for women, and bust, waist and hip measurements describe a woman&rsquo;s shape better. A high shoulder-to-waist ratio for a woman usually points to an <a href="/inverted-triangle-body-shape/">inverted triangle</a> or <a href="/hourglass-body-shape/">hourglass</a> shape; check yours with the <a href="/">body shape calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="improve" aria-labelledby="improve-h">
    <h2 id="improve-h">How to Improve Your Shoulder-to-Waist Ratio</h2>
    <p><strong>Lose waist fat:</strong> a modest calorie deficit with enough protein trims the waist while keeping muscle. See where you are on the <a href="/body-fat-percentage-chart/">body fat percentage chart</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>Build the shoulders and back:</strong> overhead presses and lateral raises widen the deltoids; pull-ups and rows widen the upper back. Train them two or three times a week and add weight over time.</p>
    <p><strong>Check the result:</strong> try different shoulder and waist sizes in the <a href="/body-visualizer/">3D body visualizer</a>, or see which build you have with the <a href="/male-body-type-calculator/">male body type calculator</a>.</p>
  </section>

